p tens), folding is not optimal. The opponent's betting pattern suggests either a straight or strong two-pair. A raise might fold out bluffs but get called/re-raised by straights. Calling is safest to see showdown with excellent pot odds.\n\nReason:\nWith trip tens and getting approximately 11:1 pot odds (risking 600 to win ~6600), I only need about 8% equity to make calling profitable. While the opponent's aggression suggests they might have a straight (J-9), there's also a reasonable chance they have two pair, a weaker set, or are bluffing a missed flush draw. Calling allows me to see showdown at a good price without risking my entire stack. Raising could turn my hand into a bluff-catcher and inflate the pot unnecessarily if I'm behind.\n\nAnswer: 0"}
